  So there is no server issuing ids, no storage, and no place for shared state to live. All three have to be designed.

  First question, the one everything else hangs off:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the user id is [3mauthoritative[0m (an identity the backend vouches for) or [3mclient-minted[0m (an identifier
  this app invents). These are not two spellings of the same thing.

  - [1mBackend-issued[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m becomes async, POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, and reads the id out of the response. Buys you an id
    that's trustworthy and matches server-side records. Costs: a real network path, async propagation through every
    caller, error/timeout handling, and a backend contract you'd need to tell me about. Hard to change later — it sets
    the shape of [38;5;153mlogin[39m permanently.
  - [1mClient-minted[0m — the app generates a UUID at login and carries it. Buys you something working today with no backend.
    Costs: it identifies a [3mbrowser session[0m, not a person; two devices are two ids, and it can't be trusted for anything
    security-relevant. Cheap to swap for a real id later [1mif[0m we keep the id behind an accessor rather than sprinkling it
    around.
  - [1mStubbed now, backend-shaped[0m — design the async, backend-issued interface, but have the stub return a fake id. Buys
    the permanent shape now and defers the backend. Costs: async complexity for a call that doesn't need it yet.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m The expensive, hard-to-reverse decision here is whether [38;5;153mlogin[39m is async and whether the
  id is authoritative — not where the bytes come from today. Committing to that shape now, against a stub, means the
  real backend later is a body swap instead of a refactor of every caller.
